  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nSurface low pressure tracking north of the Great Lakes glides \neastward today and will drag a cold front east across the forecast \narea tonight into Monday morning. Rain showers will sweep west to \neast across the region with the cold front before exiting on Monday \nmorning. Northwest flow over Lake Erie behind the front will provide \na window of lake effect rain showers Monday afternoon. Rain showers \nwill transition to a rain/snow mix Monday evening and eventually \nsnow showers Monday night as 850mb temperatures fall to -6 to -8C \nover the lake. Snow accumulations of a half inch or less will be \npossible across inland locations of Geauga, Ashtabula, Erie (PA), \nand Crawford (PA) Counties Monday night into Tuesday. Current REFS \nprobabilities of >0.5\" of snowfall peak around 40-60% across \nportions of the Snowbelt. Canadian high pressure will move overhead \non Tuesday allowing for any lingering showers to diminish. \n\nLow temperatures in the upper 30s to lower 40s tonight will fall \ninto the mid 20s across inland locations and low 30s closer to the \nlakeshore on Monday night. Highs on Monday will be seasonable in the \nmid to upper 40s for Monday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nAforementioned Canadian high pressure will provide a period of dry \nand cooler conditions to the region Tuesday and Wednesday. The high \nwill push to the east during the day Wednesday as a coastal low \npressure systems tracks north along the Atlantic Coast into the Mid-\nAtlantic and Lower Ohio Valley regions. Model guidance is beginning \nto become more aligned with this late week system with most medium \nrange members showing precipitation arriving to the Ohio Valley by \nthe afternoon on Thanksgiving. Precipitation chances will persist \nthrough Friday as the surface and upper level low gradually push \nnortheast towards New England. Highs largely remain in the 40s \nthrough next week with lows in the 20s early next week warming into \nthe 30s by mid/late week.",
